{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And the days of Isaac were</b> — there is no chronological precedence in the Torah; the recognition of Joseph preceded the death of Isaac — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 35:29). I have already written (Ramban on Genesis 11:32) that this is the practice of Scripture in all the generations: it narrates the matter of a person and his descendants and his death, and then begins with the descendants of the next generation. It would have been fitting to precede the death of Isaac before the descendants of Jacob, as it did with Abraham and all the earlier generations, but Scripture intends to tell that Isaac died at a good old age, old and full of years, after his blessed son who inherits his distinction had returned to him, and that Esau and Jacob his sons, the great ones of the world, buried him with honor. There was no need to mention that they buried him in the cave of Machpelah, because it mentioned that he was in that city, and where else would they bury him, but in the grave of his father."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:00.212666439Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}